\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A workflow net with a silent transition. Circles are places, white boxes visible transitions, and the black box a silent transition ${"τ}$. The token in the source place $i$ is a truck before the gate. The silent transition skips the yard stop, so the net also accepts a visit without any stop.}}{10}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces A real ProSiT tree: the arrival model learned from the CTB training partition (ProSiT 1.0.4, Section~\ref {sec:exp_baseline}). The tree predicts the gap until the next truck arrives, in open minutes of the arrival calendar, from the hour and weekday of the previous arrival. Each leaf holds a fitted distribution. The simulator follows the conditions from the top to one leaf and draws the gap from that leaf. The means are those of the values the simulator draws. Sunday is closed in the arrival calendar, so no gaps are drawn there.}}{20}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Steps of one simulated visit in the final model and where each value comes from. Blue: learned by ProSiT from the training log. Orange border: adaptation to the terminal (Section~\ref {sec:sim_adaptations}). Grey: structure and output.}}{36}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Control flow of the simulation model: HM 0.7 as discovered in ProM (22 places, 11 visible and 34 silent transitions, the silent ones drawn as black bars). Each silent transition stands for one dependency between two activities; the loops at RMG receive, RMG delivery and HO2 receive allow repeated stops of one kind.}}{38}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Arrival tree: minutes to the next Gate In by weekday (0 = Monday) and hour of the last arrival. Each leaf shows the training values (bars) and the fitted distribution (line), each on its own scale.}}{41}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Routing tree of the transition \texttt {RMG\_receive} at the first decision after Gate In: probability that it fires when enabled, by manifest attributes.}}{41}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces First stop of a visit by flow type: share in the training log and probability under the learned routing (normalised weights of the enabled transitions, averaged over 8,000 training manifests).}}{41}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Waiting-time tree of block T13 (final model): minutes from a free lane to the start of the stop. All leaves on one scale.}}{42}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Execution-time tree of RMG receives (final model). The first splits are on the number of other trucks at the block when the stop starts.}}{43}{figure.caption.28}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Flow of the experiments E1 to E3. Each stage reduces the set of candidate models and passes it on. E4 and E5 continue with the configuration selected in E3.}}{47}{figure.caption.32}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Share of fitting visits and precision of the discovered nets on the training log (ProM 6.15). Left: all nets. Right: the nets with more than 91\,\% fitting visits and a precision above 0.7. Hollow markers: unsound nets, values for diagnosis only. The prefix tree accepts exactly the training sequences and serves as a reference.}}{75}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Which training sequences each sound candidate accepts. Top: share of the training visits per sequence (logarithmic scale), from the most to the least frequent sequence. Middle: number of stops of each sequence. Bottom: one row per net; a coloured cell means that the net accepts the complete sequence. The six most frequent sequences cover 88.5\,\% of the visits, so a net that accepts few sequences can still fit most visits.}}{76}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Turnaround time, observed and simulated (final model; replayed visits and observed blocks; five seeds pooled).}}{91}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Median turnaround time per Gate-In hour over the test period, observed and simulated with the same trucks (seed 42).}}{92}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Load profiles, observed and simulated, in the test period (solid) and the training period (dotted); medians, means over five seeds. With three lanes, the states ``4+ trucks at the block'' and ``3+ other trucks'' cannot occur in the simulation.}}{92}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Where the time of a visit is spent, observed and simulated (means).}}{92}{figure.caption.57}%
